{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":1285,"output":5016,"cacheRead":23552,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4946,"totalTokens":29853,"cost":{"input":0.00019275,"output":0.002508,"cacheRead":0.00070656,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00340731}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I have a good picture. Design `src/doc_audit.rs`:\n\nKey data structures:\n- `DocAuditConfig`: parse `doc_audit.toml` — per-tool entries: repo path, binary name (default = tool name), optional `surface = \"none\"`.\n- `SurfaceExtraction`: run binary `--help`, harvest tokens: subcommands, long flags (`--foo-bar`), exit codes mentioned (\"Exit codes: 0, 1, 2\" or \"Exit status\"). Normalize tokens: kebab/snake/spacing → normalize by lowercasing, replacing `-` and `_` and spaces... Actually \"substring-normalized matching per D1\" — normalize token to canonical form: lowercase, remove `-`, `_`, spaces? That maps `foo-bar` and `foo_bar` and `foo bar` to `foobar`. That's simplest for normalization equivalence.\n- Inventory scanner: scan surfaces: README.md, docs/**, llms.txt, AGENTS.md managed blocks → per-surface token sets. Tokens harvested from docs: probably extract code spans/backticks and headings? For matching feature tokens we need tokens from docs; simplest: tokenize doc text into words and also treat backtick spans as tokens, then normalize the same way. Then \"token found in docs\" = normalized surface token present in doc token set.\n\nReachability: token found in docs (README/docs) but not reachable from llms.txt or AGENTS.md managed block → ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE. Token absent from all surfaces → ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED.\n\nReport wiring: audit rows as JSON rows compatible with `aggregate_rows`? The brief says \"audit rows flow into the existing report as new check-codes ... report_version stays 1 (additive); tests/report_aggregation.rs expectations pass unchanged.\" So emit report rows: `{kind: \"trial\"? , report_version: 1, scenario: \"doc-audit\", tool: <tool>, status: \"failed\", checks: [{name: ..., passed: false, code: \"ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\"}]}`. The aggregator accepts any code string (FaultKey::Agent), so no change needed in report.rs besides... hmm, brief lists `src/report.rs — accept the two new check-codes (additive only)`. The aggregator already treats codes as opaque strings. Maybe scenario_channel needs \"doc-audit\" scenario mapping? Wait — aggregate_rows errors if scenario has no declared channel. So we need to add a channel for doc-audit scenarios. `scenario_channel` maps names → channel. We could register `\"doc-audit:<tool>\"` → some channel like \"docs\". That's an additive change in report.rs's scenario_channel. Actually scenario_channel returns Option<&'static str> matching fixed names. Add a prefix check: names starting with \"doc-audit\" → Some(\"docs\"). Hmm but channel list — \"managed-block\" mentioned. Use \"docs\" channel. Keep additive.\n\nActually simplest: doc-audit emits rows with scenario name `doc-audit` (or per-tool `doc-audit-wai`), channel \"artifact\"? Existing channels: envelope, suggestions, artifact. Maybe map doc-audit* → \"artifact\"? But that mixes semantics. I'll add \"docs\" channel returned by a prefix rule in scenario_channel.\n\nCLI: `doc-audit` subcommand: reads doc_audit.toml at repo root, for each tool: extract surface (unless surface=none), scan repo docs, emit rows JSON + provenance. Exit 0 unless loud errors? Brief: dogfood `cargo run -- doc-audit` → non-empty inventory, zero silent skips. Exit code: should nonzero on undocumented features? The gate is `just tier0` wired — hmm, brief item 6: \"CLI: evallerina doc-audit subcommand; wired into just tier0\". If it exits nonzero on real undocumented features, tier0 would fail. Follow-up threshold: real rows > ~40 → STOP. Risky: if we wire real ecosystem audit into tier0 and there are real undocumented features, tier0 fails. The brief says step 6: \"Wire report rows (additive) + CLI + doc_audit.toml for the real ecosystem; just tier0 green. Dogfood check: audit run against evallerina's own surfaces produces non-empty inventory with zero silent skips.\"\n\nHmm — wiring into just tier0 but tier0 green. If audit rows exist for real ecosystem, gate fails? Maybe tier0 wiring runs the audit against evallerina itself only? Or gate fails only on... Let me think: probably tier0 runs `cargo run -- doc-audit` and the exit code is success as long as audit ran (measurement, not gate), with silent skips = loud error. Similar to report: \"Findings do not affect the exit code — the dashboard is the record\". So doc-audit exit nonzero only on loud errors (config parse failure, non-zero... no). And \"zero silent skips\": provenance recorded for surface=none and binary_absent; exit fails if any silent skip would occur? Actually provenance IS the record; the dogfood meter checks zero silent skips meaning every tool produced either tokens or provenance.\n\nBut \"wired into just tier0\" and \"the moment docs drift\" flags — brief Why says: \"after this lands, just tier0 flags undocumented or unfindable features across the ecosystem the moment docs drift.\" That suggests gate behavior — failing on rows. But then tier0 green at ship time requires real ecosystem currently clean... risky with follow-up threshold. Given threshold stop condition, likely docs are mostly fine? Uncertain. Safer design consistent with both: emit rows; exit SUCCESS if audit ran completely (all tools accounted: tokens or provenance), FAILURE only on loud errors. Wait but then it doesn't \"flag\" anything in tier0. Hmm.\n\nCompromise matching precedent (routing_parity exits nonzero on fail rows): make doc-audit exit nonzero on audit fault rows, but scope the tier0 gate to... Actually, let me reconsider: maybe gate on real ecosystem WILL produce rows > threshold → STOP condition triggers → \"ship the mechanism against fixtures, and leave real-tool onboarding notes for 73q\". The brief anticipates this! So: implement mechanism fully, run against real ecosystem; if rows > ~40, record research notes, don't tune. But then just tier0 green — if real rows exist, tier0 fails. Conflict.\n\nResolution: just tier0 wiring — run doc-audit and require: non-empty inventory for evallerina's own surfaces (dogfood) + zero silent skips, but audit fault rows are printed as the record without failing exit (measurement-first like report). That keeps tier0 green while still surfacing rows. And \"flags ... the moment docs drift\" — the rows appear in tier0 output. I'll go with: exit nonzero only on loud errors (missing config, unreadable repo); rows and provenance printed as JSON; plus a guard: if inventory is empty or any silent skip detected → nonzero (anti-goal enforcement).\n\nHmm, but \"zero silent skips\" — by design skips always produce provenance, so silent skips can't occur unless bug. Fine.\n\nNow token harvesting from --help output:\n- Lines like \"Commands:\" sections list subcommands (indented names).\n- Long flags: regex `\\s(--[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*)`.\n- Exit codes: \"Exit code\" / \"Exit status\" sections/lines listing codes. Parse patterns like \"exit code N\" or lines \"  0   success\". Keep simple: capture numbers following \"exit\" mention? Brief: \"documented exit codes where --help lists them\". Implement: if help contains a line matching /exit (code|status)/i, harvest integer tokens \"exit-<n>\"? Hmm tokens are feature tokens; exit codes could be tokens like \"exit-2\". Keep it: normalize `exit 2` → token `exit2`.\n\nNormalization: canonical(token) = lowercase, strip chars `-`, `_`, ` `. So \"foo-bar\" == \"foo_bar\" == \"foo bar\" == \"foobar\". Substring-normalized matching per D1 — hmm \"substring-normalized\": maybe matching is substring-based. I'll implement `token_set_contains(set, token)` checking exact normalized match OR substring? Keep exact normalized match, that's what test 1 requires.\n\nInventory scanning surfaces:\n- README.md (root)\n- docs/**/*.md\n- llms.txt (root)\n- AGENTS.md managed blocks (`<!-- WAI:START -->...<!-- WAI:END -->`? Generic: `<!-- \\w+:START -->` blocks). \n\nPer-surface token sets: extract candidate tokens from doc text: words in backticks, bullet-lead words, headings? Simplest robust: extract all \"code spans\" (`...`) plus all words. Then normalized word set. For fixture test: README contains \"deploy\" and \"--dry-run\" etc.\n\nReachability breadcrumbs: tokens appearing in llms.txt or managed blocks form the reachable set. Token in docs surfaces (README/docs/*.md) but not in breadcrumb set → ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE. Token not in any surface → ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED.\n\nConfig doc_audit.toml format:\n```toml\n[wai]\nrepo = \"../wai\"\nbinary = \"wai\"\n\n[openspec]\nrepo = \"fission-ai/openspec\"\nsurface = \"none\"\n```\nWait \"surface: none tools skip the binary step and are recorded in provenance\". For upstream tools without local checkout, what? openspec repo = \"fission-ai/openspec\" is not a local path... routing.toml has that. For doc_audit, surface=none means don't run binary. But still scan repo docs? openspec repo isn't checked out locally. Hmm. Keep mechanism: `surface = \"none\"` → no binary probe, provenance `skip` (record \"surface_none\"). Then inventory scan of repo path — if path doesn't exist → provenance? For upstream entries maybe mark provenance too. Let me define statuses:\n- extracted: binary ran, tokens harvested\n- skip: surface=none (provenance records reason \"surface_none\")\n- binary_absent: binary not found on PATH / repo path — loud provenance row, not silent.\n\nFor repo scanning of nonexistent local path: provenance `skip` with reason \"repo_absent\"? The brief only mandates surface=none and binary_absent. I'll add repo_absent as another explicit provenance kind (never silent). Good.\n\nPer-tool audit row set:\n- For each surface token from extraction missing in inventory → ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED (check row: name \"surface-token:<token>\"?). Actually rows: audit rows: `