s*[:=]?\\s*(\\d+)` → token \"exit<n>\". Also sections \"Exit codes:\" listing numbers.\n\nKeep it modest; fixture-friendly, general rules only.\n\nInventory scan:\n```rust\npub struct Inventory { pub surfaces: BTreeMap<String, BTreeSet<String>>, pub breadcrumbs: BTreeSet<String> }\npub fn scan_inventory(repo: &Path) -> Result<Inventory, String>\n```\nsurfaces keys: \"README.md\", \"docs/x.md\", ... plus \"llms.txt\", \"AGENTS.md\" as surfaces too? Brief: per-surface token sets for README.md, docs/**, llms.txt, AGENTS.md managed blocks. Breadcrumb sources: llms.txt + managed blocks. So breadcrumbs = tokens(llms.txt) ∪ tokens(managed blocks of AGENTS.md).\n\nToken extraction from markdown text: collect code spans `` `...` `` (each span may contain multiple words → split), plus plain words. Just: strip code fences? Simpler: take whole text, extract candidate tokens = every alphanumeric-ish word: split on non-alphanumeric? But `--dry-run` in docs → words \"dry\",\"run\". The surface token is \"dryrun\" canonical. Mismatch! So docs tokenization must also treat `--dry-run` as token \"dryrun\". If I split text on non-alnum, \"--dry-run\" yields \"dry\",\"run\" — not \"dryrun\". Hmm.\n\nAlternative normalization: canonical keeps letter sequences joined? Can't join arbitrary words.\n\nBetter: extract tokens from docs as: code spans (backtick content), inline flags regex `--[a-z0-9-]+`, and backquoted subcommand names. Plus headings/bold? For robust matching of \"deploy\" appearing as plain word in README — plain word \"deploy\" → word \"deploy\" canonical \"deploy\" matches. Good since split on non-alnum gives \"deploy\".\n\nFor \"--dry-run\" appearing in docs as code span or plain: regex `--[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*` over raw text catches it → canonical \"dryrun\". Matches surface token \"dryrun\". \n\nAnd subcommand \"tear-down\" in docs: often in backticks or as \"tear-down\" hyphenated word → word-split gives \"tear\",\"down\". Miss. Add regex for hyphenated identifier `